From 37a3e08c930542aed03d3fdd2deff20d6be6aab8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:53:01 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: decode system call arguments by syscall family

The recorder alone captures a pointer argument only when it knows the
extent of what it points to; every other pointer is recorded as an
address with a no-decoder gap. Add structured decoders for the families
whose memory is the interesting part of the call, written against the
pinned kernel's own handlers so that what is captured is what the
kernel read or wrote: message and socket calls (including socket
options by level and name, mmsg and ancillary data), extended
attributes, signal masks and signal frames (rt_sigreturn with its FPU
and xstate, uretprobe), poll, select and epoll, futex, sched
attributes, clone and clone3, process_vm_readv/writev (remote segments
are address-only: the other process's memory is never read), System V
IPC and message queues, memory policies, seccomp and classic BPF
programs, file handles, the new mount API and mount, quotactl,
kexec_load, rseq, capabilities, Landlock, LSM attributes, fcntl,
arch_prctl, prctl, ptrace, keyctl, sysfs and bpf (the attribute block
and each command's nested buffers, with sizes from the map object).

A decoder runs at entry and at exit, may own a call's whole capture,
and returns the pointer arguments it recognised so the recorder does
not report them as gaps. Selectors and layouts it does not know keep an
explicit no-decoder gap; writes the kernel makes later, in the child or
at its exit (clone's child tid, a robust list, rseq updates), are marked
asynchronous rather than guessed. capture_api.h is the interface the
recorder gives decoders: fields, strings, loss, and per-call state kept
from entry to exit; the status codes move there from recorder.c.

Hooked kernel file:
- ipc/sem.c hands its own copy of a semctl GETALL or SETALL array to
  the recorder (ant_monitor_operand()), because only the kernel knows
  the array's length (sem_nsems). The copy is bounded and recorded at
  exit as kernel-imported, only after the copy to user succeeded.

Every decoder is keyed by the system call's __NR_ name; legacy calls
the asm-generic architectures lack (stat, poll, select, signalfd,
sysfs ...) are guarded with #ifdef __NR_<name>. What only x86
has is in capture_x86.c, which native_capture.c asks for any call it has
no family for, and capture_ptrace() for every request first
(capture_api.h): arch_prctl, modify_ldt and uretprobe, the signal frame
rt_sigreturn reads from the user stack, and the ptrace requests whose
operand is an x86 structure or that only x86 defines.

Contract: the field layouts are those of revision 17; a host that does
not know a key shows it as an unknown field.
